<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="ninety-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px; margin:0 auto;">
      <h2 id="ninety-heading" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What to Expect in the First 90 Days After Launch</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
        The first two weeks after an automation goes live are the ones that matter most. Real data behaves differently from test data, no matter how thorough the testing was, and the first edge case that wasn't in the sample set usually shows up during that window. We watch the workflow closely, not just for outright failures but for smaller mismatches, like an invoice format we hadn't seen before or a lead source the routing logic didn't expect.
      </p>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
        By week four, most workflows have settled into a steady pattern and the exceptions slow down. That's usually when we sit down again, in person in Business Bay or over a call, and look at what the automation has actually processed: how much time it's saved, where it's still routing things to a person, and whether it's worth expanding to a second workflow. A lot of our second and third projects with a client come out of this conversation rather than a fresh sales pitch.
      </p>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
        By day 90, the workflow should be running with minimal oversight, and the post-launch monitoring period ends. If you want ongoing maintenance beyond that, someone checking the automation regularly, updating it as your tools change, or building the next one, we offer retainers scoped to that. Plenty of Dubai clients don't need one; the automation just keeps running.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

